From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Stockdale <jstockdale@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 03 Nov 2026 17:00:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: sc8547_svooc: move the policy arithmetic into
 a library with KUnit

The SuperVOOC session driver decides how much current the battery, the
board and the pumps allow with arithmetic that owes nothing to the
adapter protocol: the linear derates (the hotter pump die from 60 to
70 °C, each thermistor from its soft to its hard limit, the cell from
38 to 44 °C with a 1.5 A floor, 2.5 A below 21 °C), the taper (4.0, 3.0,
2.0 and 1.5 A from 4.40, 4.42, 4.44 and 4.46 V), the entry ladder (6.2 A
up to 55 %, 3 A below 85 %, 2 A above) under the 55 W budget at the top
of the VBUS window, the admissibility gate (health Good, below 90 %,
15 to 44 °C, 3.3 to 4.4 V, every thermistor below its soft limit), the
running checks (VBUS within -0.1/+0.7 V of twice the gauge's battery
voltage, the total within 0.3 A of the limit, 3.3 A per pump and alone,
60 W, the two pumps' VBUS within 250 mV twice) and the second pump's
join rule (1.4 A, then 0.5 A each within 1 A). A PD PPS back-end for the
same pumps needs exactly this envelope, and the envelope is the part
that has run: 53.9 W for 445 s and 54.2 W taking the 4.40 V taper down
to 36.7 W from the 80 W adapter (devices/iceland/charging.md). Written
twice, or edited while a second protocol is fitted around it, a
validated number drifts. The PD PPS plan (revision 2) lists these
pieces as adapter-independent (section 3.2), asks for them as a
KUnit-pinned library before any protocol work (sections 4.1 and 4.3)
and, after review item F-3, insists that the running window stay
referenced to the gauge voltage rather than to the pump's own sense.

Move that arithmetic into sc8547_direct_policy.c as pure functions on
plain integers, the gauge's battery reading and the pumps' samples, and
call them from the driver. The bodies are the former static functions',
under new names: scale, thermal_ua, taper_ua, vbat_uv, admissible,
entry_ua, regulate_target_ua, running_ok, second_join and second_shared.
The one addition is a guard in entry_ua() and regulate_target_ua()
against a divisor that is not positive (a gauge reading at or below
-0.35 V; no VBUS): each returns 0 there, which the caller's existing
refusal below 1.5 A takes, instead of dividing by zero, which the arm64
division answers with 0 and the host test would trap on. The driver
cannot reach either: the running window of running_ok() is checked
before the regulation, and the gauge reads a real cell. The driver keeps
what depends on the protocol or on time around the calls: the hotter die
of the pumps in use, the single-pump 3.3 A cap at entry, the check of
the measured current against the applied one after the ramp, the 300 to
500 ms join qualifier, the rate limits of a change, and the hard stops
of svooc_check(). The seven constants move into the library's header,
the thermistor table into the library, exported, and names are given
to the 90 % end, to the two hard stops of svooc_check() (the cell at
44 °C and the pump die at 70 °C, where the two derates also end, so
that each number exists once and the driver's stops read the library's)
and to the 54.5 W the over-power step settles the power to. running_ok()
carries the counter of pump disagreements for its caller, so that the
two-in-a-row rule keeps its place among the checks.

KUnit tests under CONFIG_SC8547_DIRECT_POLICY_KUNIT_TEST pin every
number above, the 55 W arithmetic at 8.0, 9.0 and 9.6 V (6.2, 6.0 and
5.6 A) and the over-power step (6.2 A at 9.0 V asks for 5.9 A; 6.25 A
at 8.8 V is 55.000000 W and takes no step, one microampere more does),
the 60 W fault at its boundary (6.25 A at 9.6 V passes, one microampere
more fails), the second pump's 3.3 A on both sides, the 250 mV
disagreement on both sides with its count forgiven by one agreeing
sample, the 100 mA rounding, the gauge reference of the window (a pump
VBAT of 3.0 V under a gauge at 4.0 V does not move it; a gauge at 3.0 V
does), the order of the disagreement count and the second pump's
switching check (-EIO on the first disagreeing sample with the second
pump stopped, -ERANGE on the second), the two division guards, and the
limit values themselves: 13 cases, run on the host under UML with
tools/testing/kunit/kunit.py, all passing. The library follows the
oplus_svooc.c pattern: a hidden tristate selected by the driver, a test
symbol that selects the library.

No behaviour changes. The moved bodies were compared with the originals
mechanically, after the renaming of the move (the constants, the
functions, and the parameters that replace the session's fields): what
differs is the signatures, the two guards, and the tail of each range
where the driver's own code goes on; every comparison, every operand
and its type, and the order of every early return are the original's,
and KUnit pins every number of the moved ranges.

Signed-off-by: John Stockdale <jstockdale@gmail.com>
